keel-nextcloud-19.0 (2) keel; urgency=medium

  * conf.d/main is executable. fab runs only an executable conf script, so
    the image 19.0-1 had no Nextcloud code, kept the build tools in
    /usr/local/lib/keel-build and the check page of lnmp (40lnmp-check,
    keel_check), and answered 503 (commissioning, 2026-11-12). A test now
    checks the modes of the tree.
  * The instance description needs no secret of the manifest: 00declarative
    of inithooks refuses a secret name that is not root, db or app, and
    the preseeded first boot of 19.0-1 stopped there. The first boot takes
    the secret and the password salt of Nextcloud from keel when keel gives
    them, else from the table keel_settings of the Nextcloud database, else
    it makes them, and stores them there. The seed and the replication of
    MariaDB carry them to the replica. The two Redis passwords are made on
    each node. The manifest declares secret and salt as generate: allowed
    and no Redis password.
  * keel-init asks the Nextcloud password: the hook no longer defers when
    only the shared secrets were missing, which no first boot gave.
  * The gate writes the secret and the salt of the database into
    keel-pair.config.php, and reloads PHP-FPM, before the node opens and
    while it is closed, so a cloud replica that made its own site at its
    first boot gets the values of the primary.
  * tests/commission.yaml: the cloud nodes of cloud simple and cloud
    advanced, with the Keel Core witness (tests/commission-core.yaml).

 -- KeelLinux maintainers <admin@keellinux.org>  Thu, 12 Nov 2026 18:00:00 +0000

keel-nextcloud-19.0 (1) keel; urgency=medium

  * The first layer of Nextcloud on lnmp (handbook decision 0087):
    Nextcloud 35.0.2 from the upstream tarball, pinned by sha256 and
    checked against the signature of the key "Nextcloud Security"
    (28806A878AE423A28372792ED75899B9A724937A).
  * Redis 8 of Debian on its socket for the distributed cache, the file
    locks and the PHP sessions, APCu for the local cache. Two ACL users,
    the second for the session keys only; log level 3.
  * Only the node that takes writes serves: keel-nextcloud-gate opens the
    site when read_only is 0, keel's role file says role=primary and the
    VIP of the role file is on the node, at each address event and every
    5 s; it empties Redis when the node becomes the writer, and a replica
    copies the version of the code after an upgrade of the primary.
  * The first boot writes keel-pair.config.php from four shared secrets,
    installs Nextcloud on a primary or a standalone node, writes the whole
    config.php of a replica, and asks the administrator password at a
    console through bin/nextcloud.py.
  * Behind Anubis the route passes the scheme of the client to Nextcloud
    with $keel_web_https of keel-web 0.4.4, so a request to port 443 gets
    https:// links and port 80 keeps http:// links (decision 0079).
  * The front of keel-web: the paths of the sync clients go past Anubis,
    and a plugin of the rule set allows the DAV methods on the DAV paths
    and does not read the body of a DAV PUT.

 -- KeelLinux maintainers <admin@keellinux.org>  Thu, 12 Nov 2026 03:00:00 +0000
